THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

An Orbital deployment runs a number of independently developed and
independently licensed systems. A full inventory with licenses lives in
docs/reference/third-party-licenses.md.

Note on GPL compatibility
-------------------------

Several bundled components are licensed under GPLv2 or GPLv3 — most
notably Asterisk (GPLv2 with a linking exception), Kamailio (GPLv2 or
later), and rtpengine (GPLv3). GPLv2-only and AGPLv3 are famously
incompatible *as a combined work*.

Orbital does not create a combined work with any of them. Each runs as a
separate program, in its own container, with its own address space and
lifecycle. Orbital communicates with them only over documented network
and IPC interfaces:

  - Asterisk   — AMI (TCP), ARI (HTTP), ODBC realtime tables, and
                 generated configuration files
  - Kamailio   — the binrpc/jsonrpc control interface and generated
                 dispatcher lists
  - rtpengine  — the ng control protocol (UDP)
  - LiveKit    — its HTTP/WebSocket server API (Apache-2.0 regardless)

This is aggregation of separate programs on a distribution medium, which
the GPL FAQ addresses directly and which does not cause either license to
reach across the process boundary. No GPL-licensed code is compiled,
linked, or statically embedded into the Orbital application.

Configuration files that Orbital generates for these systems are the
output of Orbital's own templates and are covered by the AGPL as part of
Orbital, not by the licenses of the programs that consume them.

If you modify Orbital and redistribute it, this reasoning is yours to
maintain: keep the process boundaries intact. Vendoring Asterisk source
into the application, or linking against its libraries, would change the
analysis.
